WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.382
The notification service sends a text for every authorized payment.

2
00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:12.484
Each send takes about 200 milliseconds, because the SMS provider is slow.

3
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.664
In a consumer group, each partition has exactly one owner.

4
00:00:16.664 --> 00:00:20.936
Here c3 reads the whole partition, and the other two sit idle.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:29.333
The real topic has six partitions, so at most six busy consumers, each sending five texts a second: 30 in all.

6
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:35.769
On a sale day 300 payments a second arrive, and the backlog grows by 270 every second.

7
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:37.883
A share group drops the owner.

8
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:43.797
The broker hands out records one at a time, so all three consumers read the same partition.

9
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:45.573
A poll acquires records.

10
00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:51.532
Each one is locked to its consumer for 30 seconds, and its delivery count goes up by one.

11
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.684
c2's texts go out, so it acknowledges both with accept.

12
00:00:55.684 --> 00:00:57.495
Records 2 and 3 are done.

13
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.226
c3 sends 4, but the provider times out on 5.

14
00:01:01.226 --> 00:01:05.073
Release hands 5 back, available for another try.

15
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.320
Any member can take it next.

16
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.408
c2 acquires 5 and 6, so record 5 gets its second delivery, on a different consumer.

17
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.564
Now c1 freezes in a long garbage collection pause and never answers.

18
00:01:18.564 --> 00:01:23.893
At 30 seconds the broker ends its locks, and 0 and 1 are available again.

19
00:01:23.893 --> 00:01:25.353
No rebalance needed.

20
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:29.345
c3 acquires them on their second delivery, and accepts both.

21
00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:35.061
A record that keeps failing is archived after five deliveries, so it can't bounce forever.

22
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:37.123
What you give up is order.

23
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:42.137
If 5 and 6 were two texts about one card, 6 went out and 5 never did.

24
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.186
So use share groups for independent jobs like these texts, never for a ledger.

25
00:01:47.186 --> 00:01:52.511
Any free consumer takes the next record under a lock, and per key order is the price.
